338 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433341 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433341 1077 "selection" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433341 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433334 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433341 1079 #uuid "f3856639-8cc5-5769-ab2a-9f53121ca700" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433340 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433342 1069 "q-65034" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433342 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and $L({y}_{2})=6{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use $\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x={cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x.$*" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433342 1072 #uuid "778954fe-ed65-5c0c-8ec0-4af91a3c5254" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433342 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & =3L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+6{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3({cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x)+6{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3{cos}^{2}⁡x-3{sin}^{2}⁡x+6{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =3({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433341 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433343 1069 "q-337766" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433343 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)={y}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+xy$\n\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-2x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y$\n\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433343 1072 #uuid "8d7d4653-1949-5012-bba8-de86196b5589" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433343 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the first term is ${y}^{2},$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=x,{f}_{1}(x)=-2x,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=3.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433339 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433344 1069 "q-337767" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433344 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}y$\n\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$\n\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y$" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433344 1072 #uuid "d74a3e3c-e2b0-50fc-9e27-1744209e35b1" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433344 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=3$ and ${f}_{0}(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is *not* linear. The coefficient of the second term is $y,$ which depends on $y$ and therefore fails linearity.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)={x}^{2},{f}_{2}(x)=0,{f}_{1}(x)=-1,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433345 1062 #uuid "0bacce76-2ae6-55fc-9d26-5d89fee1a2c9" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433345 1063 #edb/ref 17592186311885 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433346 1058 #edb/ref 17592186433348 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433346 1062 #uuid "8ff49c84-4dd2-5afa-9c91-bf97260b2e13" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433346 1063 #edb/ref 17592186265817 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433347 1058 #edb/ref 17592186433346 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433347 1062 #uuid "b82a8080-41c2-564b-9e71-63af3ded9cfe" 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433347 1063 #edb/ref 17592186433342 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433348 1058 #edb/ref 17592186433349 13194139534701 17592186045417 true] [17592186433348 106